```

## Caveats

- This is a native **specialization**, not the full existential search of Theorem
  1.1: the 46 exponents are supplied and the answer primes are required to be
  distinct and interval-bounded. The theorem neither guarantees these finite
  instances nor proves their hardness; inverse generation guarantees feasibility.
- The hidden arithmetic progression is a generator feature, not a conclusion of the
  paper. Track B measures whether a solver discovers and executes that compression.
- The 0/250,000 density estimate is relative to uniformly random increasing
  four-subsets of all primes in the interval. It gives an upper-resolution statement
  (“below roughly one in 250,000 was observed”), not an exact solution density and
  not a complexity proof.
- The panel did not run LLL, an optimized low-memory four-sum, quadratic-form
  algorithms, or a construction-aware scan of all 50 wheel gaps; the last is the
  intended compact route and is expected to succeed with tools.
- `canonical_key` fully normalizes the only input symmetry—reordering the 46 fixed
  summands—but does not attempt to identify unrelated Diophantine instances that
  happen to have the same solution set.
- Most importantly, no valid multi-vendor oracle attempt completed. Do not mark the
  family hardened, set G9 to pass, or emit it until the quota is restored and all
  three script-owned runs are repeated.

